<map version="1.0.1">
<node TEXT="RAG-based chatbot design"><node TEXT="modularized design of RAG" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Data ingestion"><node TEXT="Example of Conflict" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="problem"><node TEXT="Due to bank constraint, the team is not allowed to use advanced VLM, OCR-based methods, and the extraction from pdfs is not satisfying"></node><node TEXT="DS team"><node TEXT="Agent&apos;s performance cannot be improved due to bad extraction."></node></node><node TEXT="DE team"><node TEXT="They have tried their best to improve performance of extraction."></node><node TEXT="They cannot use advaned methods"></node><node TEXT="They don&apos;t have enough human resource to annotate data to train the model"></node></node></node><node TEXT="previous solutions"><node TEXT="1. Wait for model team to onboard VLM model inside the bank."><node TEXT="violate the time schedule of our product"></node></node><node TEXT="2. Build close cooperation between DE and DS so that whenever a user detects an error, they quickly investigate and fix the errors"><node TEXT="no gurantee on quality of product"></node><node TEXT="waste a lot of human resource on bug fix"></node><node TEXT="hard to do in production since every update needs to go through a long verification process"></node></node></node><node TEXT="my solution"><node TEXT="design a bi-language validation"><node TEXT="intuition"><node TEXT="all pdfs are written in English and Chinese"></node><node TEXT="the length and order of English and Chinese characters are not the same, so errors cased by format can be eleminated"></node></node><node TEXT="how to do"><node TEXT="the DE team keeps on improving their extraction method until the results of English and Chinese parts match 95% percent of contetn"></node><node TEXT="the DS team will accept the extraction if DE team provides the statistics and wouldn&apos;t blame DE team any more"></node></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Contextual Enrichment&quot; Strategy" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Contextual Chunking (Anthropic Style)"><node TEXT="Before embedding a chunk, use a Flash model to prepend a 1-2 sentence &quot;global context&quot; to it."></node><node TEXT="Original Chunk: &quot;The limit is $5,000 per day.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Enriched Chunk: &quot;Regarding the Platinum Credit Card ATM withdrawal policy: The limit is $5,000 per day.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Result: The vector for the enriched chunk is much more distinct and &quot;findable&quot; than the original snippet."></node></node><node TEXT="Synthetic Question Generation (Self-Querying)"><node TEXT="For every chunk, have an LLM generate 3-5 hypothetical questions that this chunk answers."></node><node TEXT="Mechanism: Embed these synthetic questions and map them to the same chunk ID."></node><node TEXT="Benefit: When a user asks a question, it will have a much higher cosine similarity to another question than to a raw piece of documentation."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Metadata Drift" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="The Problem: A user asks about &quot;Staff Discounts.&quot; Your Router sees &quot;Discount&quot; and goes to the Promotions database. But &quot;Staff Discount&quot; is actually an HR policy. Because of the hard decoupling, the Promotions DB returns &quot;No results,&quot; and the system fails."></node><node TEXT="The Solution (The &quot;Master Index&quot;):"><node TEXT="Instead of the Router just &quot;guessing&quot; based on the query, you create a tiny, ultra-lean Global Semantic Map during your nightly batch."></node><node TEXT="The Map: A list of every Document Title and its core &quot;Entity&quot; (e.g., &quot;Staff Benefits,&quot; &quot;Platinum Card Features&quot;) across all functional databases."></node><node TEXT="The Logic:"><node TEXT="Step 1: The Flash LLM looks at the query and the Global Map."></node><node TEXT="Step 2: It realizes &quot;Staff&quot; + &quot;Discount&quot; maps to HR_Policy_04."></node><node TEXT="Step 3: It explicitly tells the RAG: &quot;Query the HR Shard, even though the user said &apos;Discount&apos;.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="KISS Benefit: You don&apos;t need a massive central database. The &quot;Map&quot; is just a text file or a 100-row table that fits in the LLM&apos;s prompt context."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Negative Constraint&quot; Index" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Semantic Collapse"><node TEXT="Standard RAG (Vector Search) is mathematically biased toward Presence, not Absence."></node></node><node TEXT="The Example:"><node TEXT="Doc 1: &quot;To qualify, you must have a savings account.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Doc 2: &quot;This promo is not available for corporate clients.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Query: &quot;I am a corporate client, can I apply?&quot;"></node><node TEXT="RAG Failure: The vector search finds &quot;apply&quot; and &quot;corporate client&quot; in Doc 2. The LLM sees the text but might focus on the &quot;apply&quot; logic elsewhere and miss the &quot;NOT.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The Solution: The Constraint Shard"><node TEXT="The Shard: During nightly ingestion, you use a Flash LLM to extract only the &quot;Hard No&apos;s&quot; (exclusions, eligibility blocks, expiry dates) into a specific metadata field or a tiny &quot;Constraint&quot; vector shard."></node><node TEXT="The Retrieval: Every time you query a functional DB, you always pull the Top 2 constraints for that category."></node><node TEXT="The Prompt: You explicitly tell the LLM: &quot;First, check the CONSTRAINTS. If any apply, the answer is NO, regardless of what the general policy says.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="Lexical (The &quot;Keyword&quot; Guard)"><node TEXT="What it is: Traditional BM25 or TF-IDF (ElasticSearch style)."></node><node TEXT="Why it&apos;s back: Vectors are great at meaning, but terrible at exact strings."></node><node TEXT="The 2026 Use Case: If a user types a specific product code like CC-990-AX, a vector search might return CC-991-AX because they are &quot;semantically similar.&quot; Lexical search ensures you get the exact match. It is the &quot;Anchor&quot; for your RAG."></node></node><node TEXT="Symbolic (The &quot;Logic&quot; Guard)"><node TEXT="What it is: Representing knowledge as a set of Rules or a Knowledge Graph (e.g., &quot;IF customer_age &lt; 21 THEN ineligible&quot;)."></node><node TEXT="Why it matters: This is deterministic. There is zero &quot;hallucination&quot; in a symbolic check."></node><node TEXT="The 2026 Use Case: In your Agentic Architecture, before the LLM even answers, a &quot;Symbolic Checker&quot; (a small Python script or Logic Engine) looks at the user’s metadata (from their profile) and compares it against the target_entity tags in your Constraint Shard."></node><node TEXT="Efficiency: It can auto-reject a query in &lt;5ms without ever calling an expensive LLM."></node></node></node><node TEXT="How to handle the &quot;Up Index&quot;" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Coordinate Mapping: The parser identifies a superscript $^1$ not just as a character, but as a &quot;Citation Anchor&quot; at coordinates $(x, y)$."></node><node TEXT="Footnote Detection: It scans the bottom of the page (or the end of the document) for a matching index &quot;1.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Inject &amp; Wrap&quot; Technique: Instead of leaving the footnote at the bottom, your script injects the footnote text directly into the metadata of the chunk containing the index."></node><node TEXT="Example"><node TEXT="The Problem: The main text says &quot;Interest rate is 5% $^1$.&quot; The footnote $^1$ says &quot;Only for balances over $1M.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Ingestion Fix: You create a Merged Proposition."><node TEXT="Raw Chunk: &quot;Interest rate is 5%.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Constraint Metadata: {&quot;footnote_1&quot;: &quot;Only for balances over $1M&quot;}"></node><node TEXT="Enriched Proposition: &quot;Interest rate is 5% (Note: Only for balances over $1M).&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Ghost Table&quot; Hallucination" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="The Error: Tables are often flattened into a single string of text, losing the relationship between the Header and the Cell. This leads to the LLM misreading which number belongs to which category."></node><node TEXT="Review:"><node TEXT="Markdown Conversion: Never store tables as raw text. Convert them to Markdown or HTML. Markdown is the &quot;gold standard&quot; because LLMs are trained heavily on it and understand the column/row structure."></node><node TEXT="Cell-Level Metadata: For 1000x efficiency, if a table is very large, don&apos;t chunk it. Instead, use a Flash LLM to generate a Natural Language Summary of the table (e.g., &quot;This table shows the 2026 interest rates for three card types...&quot;) and store that summary alongside the Markdown table."></node><node TEXT="Assumption Check: Do not assume the LLM can &quot;reason&quot; across a split table. If a table spans two pages, your ingestion must merge them before chunking."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Contextual Chunk&quot; Blindness"><node TEXT="The Error: A small 200-token chunk about &quot;Minimum Balance&quot; might lose the fact that it belongs to the &quot;Gold Account&quot; specifically, because the &quot;Gold Account&quot; header was 2,000 tokens away."></node><node TEXT="Review:"><node TEXT="Breadcrumb Prepending: Every chunk should be &quot;decorated&quot; during ingestion."></node><node TEXT="Bad Chunk: &quot;The minimum balance is $5,000.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Good Chunk: &quot;[Document: Wealth Management] &gt; [Section: Gold Account] &gt; The minimum balance is $5,000.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="KISS Implementation: During your nightly batch, keep track of the &quot;Heading Path&quot; as you iterate through the PDF and prepend it to every child chunk. This makes the &quot;Small&quot; chunk globally unique and easier for the vector search to find."></node></node><node TEXT="version"><node TEXT="for documents, e.g. promotion that has clear end date, it is easy"></node><node TEXT="for HR policy, no clear end date, hard" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="The &quot;Superseded-By&quot; Logic (Ingestion)" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="When you ingest a new HR document, you shouldn&apos;t just add it. You need a Collision Detection step in your nightly batch."></node><node TEXT="The Problem: You have Work_From_Home_2024.pdf and you just uploaded Work_From_Home_2026_Revised.pdf."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Every document must have a document_family_id (e.g., WFH_POLICY)."></node><node TEXT="The Action"><node TEXT="1. The script detects a new upload for WFH_POLICY."></node><node TEXT="2. It finds all existing chunks in the vector DB with document_family_id == WFH_POLICY."></node><node TEXT="3. It updates their metadata: is_active: False and superseded_by: &quot;DOC_ID_2026&quot;."></node><node TEXT="4. It marks the new chunks as is_active: True."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Temporal Hierarchy&quot; Shard" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Sometimes, policies aren&apos;t fully replaced; they are Amended. (e.g., &quot;The 2024 policy stands, but Section 3 is updated as of today.&quot;)"></node><node TEXT="To handle this without a human, use a Version-Aware Graph or a Hierarchical Metadata structure:"><node TEXT="Metadata Field: effective_date: 2026-03-31"></node><node TEXT="Metadata Field: version_score: 2.0 (higher is newer)."></node></node><node TEXT="how to generate version score" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="The most robust way to create a version score from a date is to convert the date into a Numeric Unix Timestamp (the number of seconds since January 1, 1970)."></node><node TEXT="Using the Score in Your &quot;Symbolic&quot; Retrieval"><node TEXT="Once you have this score stored in your database row, your retrieval logic becomes a simple Logical Filter."></node><node TEXT="1. Group retrieved chunks by their &quot;document_family_id&quot; (e.g., &apos;WFH_Policy&apos;)"></node><node TEXT="2. Within each group, find the maximum version_score"></node><node TEXT="3. Discard any chunk where version_score &lt; max_score"></node></node><node TEXT="Advanced: The &quot;Semantic VersionRAG&quot; (2026 State-of-the-Art)"><node TEXT="If your HR policies are Amended (not fully replaced), you can use a more advanced &quot;Composite Score.&quot; Recent research (like VersionRAG, Oct 2025) suggests using a combination of Date and Change Significance."></node><node TEXT="If your nightly batch detects a change, have your Flash LLM categorize it:
Patch (0.1): Typo fixes.
Minor (1.0): Small rule changes (e.g., &quot;WFH from 2 days to 3 days&quot;).
Major (5.0): Full policy overhaul."></node><node TEXT="The Composite Score Formula:
VersionScore = (Timestamp \times 1.0) + (ChangeMagnitude \times 10^5)
This ensures that even if two docs are close in date, the &quot;Major&quot; update is always prioritized."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The Retrieval Strategy:"><node TEXT="When the retriever pulls 10 chunks, your &quot;Symbolic Gatekeeper&quot; (the Python script) runs a Strict Recency Filter:"></node><node TEXT="If two chunks have the same document_family_id but different version_score, discard the lower score immediately before the LLM even sees it."></node></node><node TEXT="Handling &quot;Implicit&quot; Conflicts (The VersionRAG Approach)"><node TEXT="If the document names don&apos;t match (e.g., Staff_Handbook.pdf vs Remote_Work_Guidelines.pdf), but they cover the same topic, this is an Implicit Conflict."></node><node TEXT=""></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Symbolic&quot; Solution for HR"><node TEXT="Since you are a GenAI Specialist at a bank, Compliance is your biggest stakeholder. Use Hard Version Gates:"></node><node TEXT="Unique Topic IDs: Assign every HR rule a unique ID (e.g., HR-BEN-001)."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Active&quot; Filter: Always append {&quot;is_active&quot;: true} to every vector query."></node><node TEXT="Conflict Resolver: If the LLM detects two conflicting rules in the context, it must be programmed to STOP and output: &quot;Conflict detected between Doc A and Doc B. Please contact HR for the authoritative version.&quot; (This is better for 1000x efficiency than a &quot;best guess&quot; hallucination)."></node></node></node><node TEXT="A. The &quot;Partial Update&quot; Error" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="The Risk: A 2026 document updates only the WFH days, but the 2024 document contains the &quot;Request Process.&quot; If you purge the 2024 doc because the 2026 doc is &quot;newer,&quot; the LLM won&apos;t know how to request WFH."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: During ingestion, use a Flash LLM to tag chunks as REPLACEMENT or ADDENDUM."></node><node TEXT="If REPLACEMENT: Purge the old version."></node><node TEXT="If ADDENDUM: Keep the old version but prioritize the new one for the specific clause."></node><node TEXT="solution"><node TEXT="The Metadata Strategy: &quot;The Multi-Level Key&quot;"><node TEXT="To solve this without manual human intervention, you must move from a simple is_active flag to a Composite Versioning Key."></node><node TEXT="The Schema for Partial Updates:"><node TEXT="Each chunk in your database needs these four specific metadata fields:"></node><node TEXT=""></node></node></node><node TEXT="Maintenance: The &quot;Upsert-by-Section&quot; Logic"><node TEXT="During your nightly ingestion, you don&apos;t just &quot;Add&quot; the new PDF. You use a Flash LLM as a Structural Parser to perform a &quot;Logical Merge.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Ingestion Workflow:"><node TEXT="Parse: The LLM identifies that the new document is an update for HR_BENEFITS."></node><node TEXT="Segment: The LLM breaks the new document into sections (e.g., SEC_MATERNITY, SEC_ANNUAL_LEAVE)."></node><node TEXT="Conflict Check (The &quot;Symbolic&quot; Step):"><node TEXT="The script searches the database for any existing chunks where doc_family_id == HR_BENEFITS AND section_id == SEC_MATERNITY."></node><node TEXT="The Logic: If it finds a match with a lower version_score, it marks the old chunk as is_active: False."></node></node><node TEXT="Preserve: Sections that were not mentioned in the 2026 update (e.g., SEC_SICK_LEAVE) remain is_active: True with their original 2024 version_score."></node></node><node TEXT="Retrieval: The &quot;Latest-of-Each&quot; Filter"><node TEXT="Because you have maintained the section_id, your retrieval logic now ensures a &quot;Seamless Truth.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="When a user asks about &quot;Maternity Leave,&quot; your retriever pulls chunks from both the 2024 and 2026 documents. Your Python De-conflictor script then does this:"><node TEXT="Group by section_id."></node><node TEXT="Sort each group by version_score (Descending)."></node><node TEXT="Take the Top 1 for each section_id."></node></node><node TEXT="Result: The LLM receives the &quot;Base Policy&quot; for the parts that haven&apos;t changed, but the &quot;New Policy&quot; for the specific clause that was updated. This is 1000x efficiency because the LLM doesn&apos;t have to &quot;reason&quot; about which date is newer—it only ever sees the &quot;Winner&quot; for each section."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Risks &amp; &quot;Missing&quot; Aspects Review"><node TEXT="A. The &quot;Linkage&quot; Error"><node TEXT="The Risk: If the 2026 update for Maternity Leave refers to a table in the 2024 &quot;General Benefits&quot; section, and you&apos;ve partitioned them too strictly, the LLM might lose the link."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Use Cross-Sectional Metadata. If a partial update is an ADDENDUM, the ingestion script should add a related_sections tag to the metadata, forcing the retriever to pull the &quot;Parent&quot; context even if the similarity score is lower."></node></node><node TEXT="B. The &quot;Implicit Section&quot; Problem"><node TEXT="The Risk: If your 2024 PDF doesn&apos;t have clear section headings, the LLM might assign different section_ids (e.g., Maternity vs. Parental_Leave)."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: During nightly ingestion, provide the LLM with a Master Taxonomy (a simple list of valid section_ids for that doc_family_id). This forces the LLM to map the text to existing categories rather than inventing new ones."></node></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="B. The &quot;Cross-Document Conflict&quot;"><node TEXT="The Risk: The &quot;Travel Policy&quot; says &quot;Fly Economy,&quot; but the &quot;Executive Perks&quot; doc says &quot;Fly Business.&quot; They have different doc_family_ids, so the Recency Filter won&apos;t catch it."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: This is where Step-Back Prompting is vital. You must retrieve the &quot;Global Hierarchy&quot; (e.g., &quot;Which policy takes precedence: Travel or Perks?&quot;) and pass that priority to the LLM." FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Example"><node TEXT="When a query arrives, you don&apos;t just search for the specific &quot;action.&quot; You search for the Hierarchy of Authority."></node><node TEXT="If a user asks about &quot;Business Class flights,&quot; the Flash-tier router generates a Step-Back Query:"><node TEXT="&quot;What is the precedence of policies for employee travel and executive perks?&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The Retrieval Logic:"><node TEXT="The system retrieves three things:"></node><node TEXT="The Specific Rule (A): Travel Policy (Economy)."></node><node TEXT="The Specific Rule (B): Executive Perks (Business)."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Tie-Breaker&quot; (The Step-Back): A &quot;General Governance&quot; chunk that says: &quot;In cases of conflict, the Executive Handbook overrides departmental policies.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The KISS Solution: &quot;Precedence Tags&quot;"><node TEXT="During your nightly ingestion, assign each document a Priority Score (1-10)."></node><node TEXT="General Employee Handbook: Priority 1"></node><node TEXT="Departmental Policy: Priority 3"></node><node TEXT="Executive/Special Policy: Priority 8"></node></node><node TEXT="The Logic: If the retriever pulls two conflicting chunks with different doc_family_ids, the Python script automatically suppresses the chunk with the lower Priority Score. The LLM only sees the &quot;Winning&quot; policy."></node></node><node TEXT="Reassurance Layer"><node TEXT="The &quot;Transparent Winner&quot; UI"><node TEXT="Instead of silently hiding the losing policy, the LLM should be instructed to acknowledge the hierarchy. This builds trust and prevents the user from feeling like the AI is &quot;guessing.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Prompt Instruction:"><node TEXT="&quot;If you find information in a higher-priority document (e.g., Executive Handbook) that contradicts a lower-priority document (e.g., General Travel Policy), explicitly state: &apos;According to the [High Priority Doc], which takes precedence over [Low Priority Doc], the rule is [X].&apos;&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="Why this helps:"><node TEXT="Traceability: The user knows why the AI chose that answer."></node><node TEXT="Auditability: If the &quot;Priority Score&quot; was set incorrectly during ingestion, a human user can see the error and report it."></node></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="Deterministic Fallback: The Conflict Trigger"><node TEXT="If the Priority Scores are equal (e.g., two different Departmental Policies contradict each other) and the Step-Back query doesn&apos;t find a tie-breaker, the system must immediately stop."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Safe Exit&quot; Protocol:"><node TEXT="Instead of letting the LLM &quot;hallucinate&quot; a middle ground, you use a Conflict Detector LLM (your Flash-tier model) to compare the retrieved chunks."></node></node><node TEXT="The Prompt for the Detector:"><node TEXT="&quot;Compare these two chunks. Do they provide conflicting instructions for the same user attribute? If YES, output CONFLICT_DETECTED. Do not attempt to resolve.&quot;"></node></node></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="Assumptions &amp; Risks" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="🛑 The &quot;Constraint Coverage&quot; Gap"><node TEXT="Assumption: You assume the Flash model can find every &quot;NOT&quot; in a policy."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: Negative logic is often buried in footnotes or &quot;Table B&quot; on page 50."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Your Parent-Child mapping must include tables. If a sentence in a chunk references &quot;See Appendix A,&quot; your ingestion script must follow that link and &quot;attach&quot; Appendix A to the chunk."></node></node><node TEXT="🛑 The &quot;User Data Freshness&quot; Risk"><node TEXT="Assumption: The user&apos;s intent is based on their profile data."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: A user might be asking for a friend or their child."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: In your Intent Recognition layer, check if the user is asking about &quot;self&quot; or a &quot;third party.&quot; If it&apos;s a third party, your Symbolic Gatekeeper must be ignored, and the system must provide general information instead of a personalized &quot;No.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="🛑 The &quot;Nightly Batch&quot; Bottleneck"><node TEXT="Assumption: A nightly update is enough."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: In banking, a promo can be &quot;Paused&quot; instantly due to a technical error."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Implement a &quot;Kill Switch&quot; Metadata Tag. Even if you don&apos;t update the whole DB, you can update a tiny &quot;Status Table&quot; that the system checks in Phase 3 (Symbolic Check)."></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="Data retrieve"><node TEXT="assumption"><node TEXT="self sufficient questions are answered"></node></node><node TEXT="trick" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="one files in one vector database"><node TEXT="use metadata (end_date) for search"></node></node><node TEXT="metadata filter"><node TEXT="extract promotion names, valid dates"></node></node><node TEXT="semantic search"><node TEXT="after metadata filter, normally we only have less than 100 chunks, a simple similarity search will give the results"></node><node TEXT="normally we even don&apos;t need technique of rerank to further select the chunks"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Missing Data&quot; Hallucination"><node TEXT="The Assumption: If the RAG retrieves $&lt;10$ chunks, the LLM will answer correctly."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: If the actual answer is not in those 10 chunks, the LLM might still try to &quot;help&quot; by hallucinating."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Add a Strict Null-Response Trigger. If the combined similarity score of the top 3 chunks is below a &quot;Grounding Threshold,&quot; the system must output: &quot;The specific details for [Intent] are not in the current documentation.&quot;"></node></node></node><node TEXT="extension" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Small-to-Big Retrieval (Parent-Child Indexing)" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Instead of indexing the full text of a policy, index small &quot;sentences&quot; or &quot;propositions&quot; (the Child), but when a match is found, return the larger &quot;paragraph&quot; (the Parent) to the LLM."></node><node TEXT="Why? Small chunks are easier for vectors to find accurately."></node><node TEXT="The Result: You get the precision of a 50-token search with the context of a 500-token answer."></node><node TEXT="implementation"><node TEXT="Sentence-Window Retrieval"><node TEXT="The &quot;Small&quot;: A single sentence. This provides very &quot;sharp&quot; vector embeddings because the semantic focus is narrow."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Big&quot;: When a sentence is retrieved, the system fetches the $N$ sentences before and after it (the &quot;window&quot;) to send to the LLM."></node><node TEXT="Why it works: It solves the &quot;lost in the middle&quot; problem. The vector search finds the exact detail (the sentence), and the LLM gets the necessary surrounding context to understand it."></node></node><node TEXT="Recursive/Parent-Child Chunking"><node TEXT="This technique creates a physical hierarchy in your database."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Small&quot; (Children): You split a document into small 100-200 token chunks."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Big&quot; (Parent): Each child chunk contains a reference (an ID) to a much larger 1000-token parent chunk or even the entire document section."></node><node TEXT="The Logic: You search the small chunks for high semantic overlap. Once the top $k$ children are found, you swap them out for their parent chunks before passing them to the LLM. This ensures the LLM has a complete view of the logic, not just a snippet."></node><node TEXT="Extension"><node TEXT="The Assumption: You assume the &quot;Parent&quot; chunk always contains the answer."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: If a sentence in the &quot;Small&quot; chunk refers to a table or image 3 pages away, the &quot;Parent&quot; chunk (usually just the surrounding paragraph) will miss it."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: During ingestion, use Multimodal Parsing (like LayoutLM or specialized PDF parsers) to extract tables into Markdown/JSON and &quot;attach&quot; them to all related text chunks."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Atomic Propositions (Decontextualization)"><node TEXT="Since you are already using a Flash model for intent recognition, you can use it here as well during the ingestion phase."></node><node TEXT="The Technique: You pass a paragraph to the LLM and ask it to break it down into &quot;Atomic Propositions&quot;—standalone sentences that don&apos;t use pronouns (e.g., instead of &quot;It offers 5%,&quot; the proposition is &quot;The Platinum Card offers 5% cashback&quot;)."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Small&quot;: These decontextualized propositions are embedded."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Big&quot;: The original paragraph."></node><node TEXT="Benefit: This dramatically improves retrieval for specific details because it removes the ambiguity of &quot;it,&quot; &quot;this,&quot; or &quot;they.&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="Query Transformation"><node TEXT="If the user’s query is messy, even the best vector DB will struggle. Since you already have an &quot;Intent Recognition&quot; layer, you can expand it into a Query Reformulator."></node><node TEXT="HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings)"><node TEXT="1. The user asks: &quot;How do I get a higher limit?&quot;"></node><node TEXT="2. Your Flash model generates a &quot;fake&quot; ideal answer: &quot;To increase your credit card limit, you must provide 3 months of salary slips...&quot;"></node><node TEXT="3. You use the fake answer to search the vector database."></node><node TEXT="4. Why? Vectors of &quot;answers&quot; are closer to other &quot;answers&quot; than &quot;questions&quot; are to &quot;answers.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="Step-Back Prompting" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="If a user asks a specific question about a promotion, have the model generate a &quot;step-back&quot; query to retrieve the broader policy first. This ensures the LLM understands the &quot;rules of the game&quot; before looking at the specific promo."></node><node TEXT="Example:A complex Credit Card Promotion"><node TEXT="User Query: &quot;Can I get the $200 cashback if I spent $5,000 on my new card yesterday, but I already have a savings account with you?&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Without Step-Back (Direct RAG)"><node TEXT="Search: The system searches for &quot;$200 cashback,&quot; &quot;$5,000 spend,&quot; and &quot;savings account.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Retrieval: It finds a chunk about the specific promo."></node><node TEXT="Risk: It might miss the Global Terms &amp; Conditions stating that &quot;New-to-Bank&quot; customers only qualify—and a savings account makes you an &quot;Existing Customer.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="With Step-Back Prompting (The &quot;1000x&quot; Way)"><node TEXT="Step 1: Query Transformation"><node TEXT="The system takes the user query and generates a &quot;Step-Back&quot; version:"></node><node TEXT="Step-Back: &quot;What are the eligibility definitions for &apos;New-to-Bank&apos; vs &apos;Existing&apos; customers for credit card promotions?&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="Step 2: Dual Retrieval"><node TEXT="The RAG pipeline now performs two searches:"><node TEXT="Narrow Search: Specific details of the $200 cashback promo."></node><node TEXT="Broad Search (Step-Back): General eligibility and &quot;New-to-Bank&quot; definitions."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Step 3: Context Assembly"><node TEXT="The LLM receives both sets of information:"></node><node TEXT="Context A (Step-Back): &quot;A customer is considered &apos;Existing&apos; if they hold any active deposit, loan, or investment account.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Context B (Narrow): &quot;Promotion X gives $200 to New-to-Bank customers who spend $5,000 in the first 30 days.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="Step 4: Final Answer"><node TEXT="LLM: &quot;No, you do not qualify. While you met the $5,000 spend requirement, our general policy defines anyone with a savings account as an &apos;Existing Customer,&apos; whereas this specific promotion is reserved for &apos;New-to-Bank&apos; individuals.&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Step-Back&quot; Template"><node TEXT="&quot;You are an AI specialized in [Domain, e.g., Banking Policy]. Given the user&apos;s specific question: &apos;[User Query]&apos;, what is the broader underlying principle or policy category I should retrieve to ensure a correct answer?&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="A. The &quot;Blind Router&quot; Assumption"><node TEXT="The Assumption: You assume the Router can see every conflict."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: If the Router only searches the Travel shard, it will never find the conflicting rule in the Executive Perks shard."></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Implement a Cross-Shard Trigger. If a query contains high-value keywords (e.g., &quot;Executive,&quot; &quot;Director,&quot; &quot;Exception&quot;), the Router must perform a broadcast search across both the specific shard and the &quot;Global Governance&quot; shard."></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Summarization Loss&quot; in 10 Rounds"><node TEXT="The Assumption: 10 rounds of history fit in the prompt for intent recognition."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: In a complex HR or Finance discussion, 10 rounds can be messy and full of &quot;corrections.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Instead of passing raw text, pass a State Object."><node TEXT="Example: {&quot;current_topic&quot;: &quot;Travel&quot;, &quot;user_status&quot;: &quot;Band 2&quot;, &quot;confirmed_destination&quot;: &quot;Singapore&quot;}."></node><node TEXT="This keeps the &quot;Intent Recognition&quot; lean and 1000x more efficient."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Missing: The &quot;Negative Reassurance&quot; (The &quot;NO&quot; Answer)"><node TEXT="The Reality: The most dangerous RAG failure is turning a &quot;No&quot; into a &quot;Maybe.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Fix: If the Constraint Shard returns a hard exclusion (e.g., is_eligible: False), the Retrieval layer must short-circuit. Do not let the LLM summarize the &quot;Benefits&quot; text; force it to lead with the &quot;Ineligibility&quot; reason and the associated authority_source."></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="Answer generation"><node TEXT="use a prompt template and fill in user query and retrieved chunks"></node><node TEXT="Output the answer together with sentences in the chunks that answer the user query"></node></node></node><node TEXT="architect improvement" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Evaluation or Debug"><node TEXT="modularized desgn makes it easy to locate errors"></node><node TEXT="e.g."><node TEXT="if the retrieved chunks don&apos;t contain the info in the answer, it is LLM hallucination"></node><node TEXT="if info related to user query is in the vector database, but not retrieved chunks, it is retrieve error."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Intention Recognition Layer" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Motivation"><node TEXT="During UAT, users report non-satisfying performance"></node><node TEXT="I arranged a meeting with business partners, and look at how they use the system."><node TEXT="just key words, short name, typos, splitted sentence in consecutive inputs"></node><node TEXT="these are noisy inputs"></node></node></node><node TEXT="Battle on methods" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Previous solution"><node TEXT="just search the vector database on user query, and then answer based on user query and retrieved contents"><node TEXT="safety first"></node><node TEXT="&quot;search engine&quot; mental model"></node><node TEXT="Avoiding the &quot;Talkative&quot; Agent"></node></node></node><node TEXT="my claim"><node TEXT="a right answer in several rounds is better than a wrong answer at once"></node><node TEXT="long time to answer"><node TEXT="retrieve is time consuming"></node></node></node><node TEXT="what intention layer do" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="fuzzy match"><node TEXT="extract name of promotion from user query and metadata of vector database"></node></node><node TEXT="ask for clarification"><node TEXT="if user query is not clear, ask for details"><node TEXT="e.g. are you asking for &lt;hot queries related to the promotion&gt;"></node></node></node><node TEXT="summarize chat with user to generate self sufficient questions for rag"></node><node TEXT="Security Silhouette&quot; (The Missing Piece)"><node TEXT="Since this is for a high-trust environment (Banking), there is one detail leaders often miss: PII Stripping in the Intention Layer."></node><node TEXT="The Optimization: The Intention Guard should act as a Redaction Proxy."></node><node TEXT="The Flow: If a user types &quot;My balance on account 12345,&quot; the Intention Layer replaces it with [ACCOUNT_ID] before passing it to the Vector DB or the LLM."></node><node TEXT="Why: This ensures that your &quot;Knowledge Base&quot; and &quot;LLM Logs&quot; never contain sensitive PII, simplifying your Compliance and Audit overhead (KISS)."></node></node><node TEXT="i.e. normalize high entropy inputs"><node TEXT="prevent &quot;garbage in garbage out&quot;"></node></node></node><node TEXT="results"><node TEXT="Business Partners&apos; Trust"><node TEXT="without any modification to rag part, we improve the accuracy of user query to lab level and regain business partners&apos; trust in two weeks"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Signal-to-Noise&quot; Breakthrough"><node TEXT="The Metric: &quot;By implementing the Intention Guard, we reduced Noisy Retrievals by &gt;50%.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Why: Most RAG failures happen because the vector search is &quot;distracted&quot; by typos or irrelevant keywords. By sanitizing the query first, the Vector DB only sees the &quot;Clean Signal.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Google&quot; Value: This shows you understand Resource Efficiency. You aren&apos;t wasting tokens or compute on &quot;guessing.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Perfect Assumption&quot; Principle"><node TEXT="This is your strongest architectural point."></node><node TEXT="The Logic: &quot;Because the Intention Layer guarantees a clean, categorized query, the downstream RAG can operate under a &apos;Perfect Assumption&apos;.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Benefit: The RAG prompt doesn&apos;t need to be 2,000 tokens long with 50 &quot;if-then&quot; cases for typos. It can be a Simple, High-Precision Prompt focused purely on the verified intent."></node><node TEXT="The Result: Higher accuracy, lower latency, and a much easier system to debug (KISS)."></node></node><node TEXT="Modular Maintenance (The &quot;Anti-Entropy&quot; Rule)"><node TEXT="The Action: &quot;We decoupled the business logic into specialized shards (Promos, Policies, APIs).&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Result: &quot;When the Bank launched a new credit card activity, we didn&apos;t have to &apos;retrain&apos; or &apos;re-index&apos; the entire system. We just added a new shard and one routing rule.&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="further optimizations" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="use small models"><node TEXT="as the user query is normally short, we don&apos;t need use advanced models to capture details in a long prompt"></node><node TEXT="analysis of costs" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="The &quot;Token Tax&quot;: Understanding the bill"><node TEXT="The &quot;Big RAG&quot; Cost (Expensive)"><node TEXT="To make a single-stage RAG &quot;smart&quot; enough to handle typos and fuzzy intent, you have to give it a huge prompt (e.g., 2,000 tokens of &quot;If the user says X, do Y&quot;).Every query costs you 2,000+ tokens, even if the user just says &quot;Hi."></node></node><node TEXT="&quot;Intention Layer&quot; Cost (Efficient)"><node TEXT="You use a Small Model (like Gemini Flash or a fine-tuned 7B model) for the Intention Layer."></node><node TEXT="Small Prompt: Only 200 tokens to identify intent."></node><node TEXT="The Routing Payoff: If the intent is &quot;Check Balance,&quot; you stop there and call an API ($0 tokens). You only trigger the &quot;Big&quot; RAG prompt for complex queries."></node><node TEXT="Calculation: 60% of queries stay &quot;Light&quot; (200 tokens). 40% go &quot;Deep&quot; (2,000 tokens). Total cost is reduced by ~50%."></node></node><node TEXT="Comparison on price"><node TEXT="Gemini 1.5 Pro: ~$1.25 per 1 million input tokens."></node><node TEXT="Gemini 1.5 Flash: ~$0.075 per 1 million input tokens"></node><node TEXT="roughly 16X"></node></node><node TEXT="Computation"><node TEXT="assumption"><node TEXT="Big RAG"><node TEXT="3000 tokens per request, suppose 1 llm call for each user query"></node></node><node TEXT="intention layer"><node TEXT="100 tokens per request, 5 llm call for each user query"></node></node></node><node TEXT="roughly 100X (16 * (3000/100/5)"></node></node></node><node TEXT="Latency vs. Throughput (The &quot;Time is Money&quot; Cost)"><node TEXT="Big RAG: Processing a 2,000-token prompt takes time (Latency). If the system is busy, users wait."></node><node TEXT="Your Layer: The Intention Layer is nearly instant because the prompt is tiny. By &quot;cleaning&quot; the query first, the subsequent RAG search is faster because it’s looking at a shorter, filtered context."></node><node TEXT="100ms of &apos;Thinking&apos; is better than 5 seconds of &apos;Guessing.&apos; By sanitizing the query, we actually save time on the downstream RAG because the prompt is smaller and the search is filtered. It’s a net-zero latency trade-off for a 20% accuracy gain.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Human-in-the-loop&quot; Cost"><node TEXT="This is the hidden cost leaders forget."></node><node TEXT="Wrong Answer Cost: If a &quot;Big RAG&quot; guesses wrong and a user calls the bank&apos;s human support line, that 10-minute phone call costs the bank $10–$20."></node><node TEXT="Graceful Failure Cost: If your Agent realizes it&apos;s confused and hands over a clean chat history to a human, the call takes 2 minutes instead of 10. You just saved the bank 80% of the support labor cost."></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="count and cache user queries" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="how to do"><node TEXT="maintain a list of hot queries"></node><node TEXT="no need to generate again, just reply user with previous generation"></node></node><node TEXT="Precision Multiplier" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Accuracy vs. Entropy"><node TEXT="The Logic: Simplicity is the ultimate Sophistication."></node><node TEXT="Brute Force (70%)"><node TEXT="A &quot;Flat RAG&quot; is fighting Semantic Entropy. It’s trying to find a needle (the answer) in a haystack of noisy user input and 20 years of legacy documents. The 30% failure rate isn&apos;t just a &quot;bad answer&quot;; it&apos;s a Loss of Trust that costs the bank millions in brand reputation and support labor."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Frustration Loop&quot;: If an AI gives a wrong or vague answer 30% of the time, the user has to call the human agent anyway. Now the bank is paying for the LLM tokens AND the Human Agent&apos;s time."></node><node TEXT="Needs a team of 10 &quot;Prompt Engineers&quot; and &quot;Data Scientists&quot; constantly tweaking a 5,000-token prompt to handle every new edge case."></node></node><node TEXT="Your Federated Logic (90%+)"><node TEXT="By using the Intention Guard, you &quot;clean the needle&quot; before looking for the haystack. By enforcing the &quot;Perfect Assumption,&quot; the downstream RAG doesn&apos;t have to be a genius; it just has to be a clerk."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Utility Threshold&quot;: At 90% accuracy, the AI becomes self-service. You aren&apos;t just saving pennies on tokens; you are saving dollars on labor."></node><node TEXT="A single Senior Architect can build the Intention Guard and the Routing Logic. Since each module (Shard/API) is isolated and operates on a &quot;Perfect Assumption,&quot; the &quot;fine-tuning&quot; is minimal."></node><node TEXT="High normalization = High cache hits. Since the &quot;Perfect Assumption&quot; forces queries into standard &quot;Canonical Forms,&quot; you can serve 30%–50% of common banking questions directly from a KV Cache or a Semantic Cache (like Redis or Vertex AI&apos;s internal caching)."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Graceful Failure&quot; (The 10% Safety Net)"><node TEXT="Since no system is 100%, how do you handle the remaining 10%?"></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Confidence Score&quot; Trigger: If the Intention Layer sees two intents with similar scores (e.g., 45% &quot;Credit Card&quot; vs. 45% &quot;Loan&quot;), it doesn&apos;t guess."></node><node TEXT="The Elegant Clarification: Instead of a robot saying &quot;Error,&quot; it says: &quot;I see you&apos;re asking about your accounts. Are you looking for Credit Card Rewards or Loan Status? I want to make sure I give you the most accurate info.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Handoff: If the user is still frustrated, the Intention Layer triggers a human handover with the &quot;Context Payload&quot; (the intent, the live API data, and the history)."></node></node><node TEXT="Analysis on costs"><node TEXT="2-3X on precision (labor reduction)"></node><node TEXT="3-5X on cache"></node><node TEXT="all combined, achieve approximately 1000X"></node></node></node><node TEXT="Canonical Key Mapping (Cache Optimization)"><node TEXT="The Optimization: Instead of caching the raw user string, cache the Intent + Entity pair."></node><node TEXT="Example"><node TEXT="User A: &quot;What&apos;s my visa deal?&quot; $\rightarrow$ Intent: Promo_Check, Entity: Visa_Infinite"></node><node TEXT="User B: &quot;Any credit card promos?&quot; $\rightarrow$ Intent: Promo_Check, Entity: Visa_Infinite"></node></node><node TEXT="Why: Both map to the same Canonical Key. If the &quot;Visa_Infinite&quot; promo hasn&apos;t changed in 24 hours, you serve the exact same cached response to both users without hitting the LLM for synthesis at all."></node><node TEXT="Impact: Increases your Cache Hit Rate exponentially, moving you closer to that 1,000x cost reduction."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Active Learning&quot; Feedback Loop"><node TEXT="The Optimization: When a &quot;Graceful Failure&quot; occurs and the user is handed to a human, the Human&apos;s Resolution should be logged back as a &quot;Gold Standard&quot; label for that intent."></node><node TEXT="Why: This allows you to &quot;Fine-tune&quot; your Intention Layer (the small model) every month using real-world failures. The system actually gets smarter the more it fails."></node><node TEXT="Impact: Converts OpEx (Human labor) into IP (Better Models)."></node></node><node TEXT="some details"><node TEXT="&quot;If you have 50 specialized shards, how does the Intention Layer know which one to pick without checking all of them?&quot;"><node TEXT="Reflex: &quot;We use a Hierarchical Taxonomy. The Intention Layer classifies the &apos;Domain&apos; first (e.g., Retail vs. Corporate). Only the relevant shards are &apos;woken up.&apos; This follows the KISS principle of &apos;minimum necessary context&apos;.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="We have a limited budget. Should we spend it on better Embeddings or a better Intention Layer?&quot;"><node TEXT="Reflex: &quot;The Intention Layer. Better embeddings can&apos;t fix a &apos;Dirty Signal.&apos; If we clean the intent first, even &apos;average&apos; embeddings perform like &apos;elite&apos; ones because they are operating in a Low-Noise Environment.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="&quot;How do you make this even better?&quot;"><node TEXT="&quot;We move from Linear Logic to Parallel Orchestration. We use the Intention Layer to trigger the &apos;Source of Truth&apos; (API) and the &apos;Knowledge Base&apos; (Vector Shards) simultaneously, then use Metadata Pre-filtering to ensure the LLM only sees the &apos;Canonical Truth.&apos; This maximizes speed, minimizes tokens, and creates a self-improving feedback loop from our human escalations.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="How do you handle the trade-off between speed and accuracy?"><node TEXT="&quot;I don&apos;t trade them; I decouple them. I use Parallel Execution to win on speed and Metadata Hard-Filtering to win on accuracy. By the time the LLM even sees the prompt, the &apos;Noise&apos; has already been filtered out by the Intention Guard and the Database Metadata. This allows us to maintain a KISS-compliant system that is fast enough for retail but accurate enough for banking.&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="Semantic Load Balancer"><node TEXT="more functionalities"><node TEXT="add other services"><node TEXT="HR policy"></node></node><node TEXT="interact with other system"><node TEXT="e.g. user profile to provide accurate suggestion"></node></node><node TEXT="combine several functions"><node TEXT="e.g. recommendation based on user profile"><node TEXT="How do you ensure the API data and the Vector DB data stay in sync if a user&apos;s status changes mid-session?&quot;" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Just-in-Time (JIT) Context Fetching"><node TEXT="The Logic: We don&apos;t store the user&apos;s &quot;Status&quot; in the Agent&apos;s memory for the whole session."></node><node TEXT="The Action: For every High-Stakes Intent (like &quot;What rewards do I have?&quot;), the Intention Layer triggers a Fresh API Call."></node><node TEXT="The Whiteboard Moment: You draw a loop from the Intention Layer back to the Core API for every turn. This ensures that if the user just upgraded their card 5 minutes ago in a different tab, the Agent sees it now."></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Semantic Validation&quot; Step (The Double-Check)"><node TEXT="The Logic: Even if the Vector DB (the Promotions) is a bit &quot;stale&quot; (e.g., it hasn&apos;t been re-indexed since the user&apos;s status changed), the Intention Layer acts as the final judge."></node><node TEXT="The Action: The LLM is instructed: &quot;Only present the promotion if the Required_Status from the Vector DB matches the Current_Status from the Live API.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Result: You&apos;ve built a Fail-Safe. Even if the search returns a &quot;Platinum&quot; deal, the Agent will catch the mismatch in the final reasoning step and &quot;Self-Correct&quot; the output."></node></node><node TEXT="Handling &quot;Transient States&quot; (The &quot;Pending&quot; Problem)"><node TEXT="The Problem: What if the status is &quot;In Progress&quot; (e.g., a pending credit limit increase)?"></node><node TEXT="The Action: The Intention Layer recognizes the &quot;Pending&quot; flag from the API and routes the user to a Status-Check Agent instead of a Recommendation Agent."></node><node TEXT="KISS Principle: Don&apos;t try to &quot;guess&quot; the future state. Report the current &quot;Ground Truth&quot; and offer a &quot;Check Back Later&quot; path."></node></node></node><node TEXT="&quot;Statelessness is the cure for synchronization headaches.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="&apos;Cold Start&apos; problem if a user has no profile data yet?&quot;"><node TEXT="Your Answer: &quot;The Intention Layer recognizes the &apos;Empty Profile&apos; state and routes the user to a General Discovery mode, or asks clarifying questions to build a temporary context—maintaining the KISS principle of always having a fallback path.&quot;"></node></node></node><node TEXT="add complex reason abilities"></node></node><node TEXT="divide and conquer"></node><node TEXT="advantages"><node TEXT="low coupling"></node><node TEXT="low latency"><node TEXT="use deterministic fuzzy match algorithms to solve typos, short names"></node><node TEXT="route simple query to API"></node><node TEXT="use small models"></node></node><node TEXT="micro-service architecture"></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Metadata Drift&quot; Assumption"><node TEXT="The Assumption: You assume metadata (tags like hr_policy) is always accurate and sufficient to filter."></node><node TEXT="The Reality: If a policy document mentions a &quot;Promotion for Employees,&quot; does it go in the HR shard or the Promotion shard?"></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Implement Overlapping Shards or a Global &quot;Master&quot; Index (very low dimension) that only stores document titles to help the Router decide which functional shard to open."></node></node></node><node TEXT="global architect" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Constraint"><node TEXT="due to government policy, the data should be strictly saved inside the local servers"></node></node><node TEXT="solution"><node TEXT="federated agent architecture"><node TEXT="Hub-and-Spoke Model"><node TEXT="Local Leaf Agents: These are the &quot;Janitors.&quot; They live next to the local &quot;S**t-Hills&quot; (Legacy DBs). Their only job is to map messy, local schemas into a Unified Semantic Protocol. They handle the &quot;dialect&quot; of the local data."></node><node TEXT="Global Orchestrator Agent: This is the &quot;CEO.&quot; It doesn&apos;t care about the underlying SQL or the local mess. It communicates only in High-Level Intent."></node><node TEXT="The Result: You achieve global consistency without moving a single byte of restricted data. You’ve built a virtual &quot;Single Source of Truth&quot; without the $100M migration cost."></node></node><node TEXT="Zero-PII Semantic Retrieval"><node TEXT="Instead of the Global Agent asking for a &quot;Customer Profile,&quot; which might trigger a privacy violation, it sends a High-Level Analytical Query to the Local Leaf."></node><node TEXT="The Process: The Local Leaf Agent performs the vector search and retrieval within the local boundary (e.g., a VPC in Germany for GDPR or Singapore for PDPA)."></node><node TEXT="The Transformation: Before sending anything back, the Local Leaf abstracts or anonymizes the findings. It might say: &quot;The customer is a High-Net-Worth individual with a preference for ESG funds,&quot; instead of sending the raw transaction log or the customer’s name."></node></node><node TEXT="Semantic Alignment"><node TEXT="To ensure the Global Orchestrator understands the local results, you use a Unified Embedding Space (like Google&apos;s Multilingual Universal Sentence Encoder). The math stays the same globally, even if the data stays local."></node></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="handle legacy data and code"><node TEXT="Runtime Logic Healing"><node TEXT="We don&apos;t fix the legacy code; we use the Agent&apos;s reasoning to &quot;patch&quot; the output based on business rules."></node></node></node><node TEXT="graceful failure"><node TEXT="fail to recognize the intention in several rounds"><node TEXT="The &quot;Graceful Failure&quot;: You trigger an automated handover to a Human Agent."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Context Payload&quot;: You don&apos;t just dump the user; you send the Chat History + the Agent&apos;s &quot;Confidence Score&quot; + the identified Intent to the human."></node><node TEXT="The Result: The human doesn&apos;t have to ask &quot;How can I help you?&quot; They say, &quot;I see you&apos;re looking for a specific Visa promotion from 2023, let me help you with that.&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="feature store at google scale" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="data storage"><node TEXT="Immutability &amp; Temporal Joins"><node TEXT="You&apos;re spot on. By adding a timestamp, you turn data into an immutable log."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Senior&quot; Signal: Because the data is immutable, you eliminate &quot;Cache Invalidation&quot; bugs. You don&apos;t &quot;update&quot; a feature; you append a new version."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Why&quot;: This allows for Reproducible Training. If a model failed three weeks ago, you can reconstruct the exact state of the world at that moment because the old timestamps were never overwritten."></node></node><node TEXT="Unified Logic (The &quot;DSL&quot; Approach)"><node TEXT="The &quot;KISS&quot; Solution: Use a Domain Specific Language (DSL)—usually a YAML or SQL-based spec."></node><node TEXT="The Execution: You define the logic (e.g., sum(purchases) over 7d) in a central registry. The Feature Store &quot;compiler&quot; then generates:"><node TEXT="A Spark/Beam job for the Offline (Batch) path."></node><node TEXT="A Flink/Dataflow job for the Online (Streaming) path."></node></node><node TEXT="The Result: You align the platforms, not the codebases. The library differences disappear because the underlying engine (like Apache Beam) is the same for both."></node></node><node TEXT="non-consistence between batch and stream"><node TEXT="using on unified framework, the difference is that data of different time is saved in different place"></node></node><node TEXT="Tiered Storage (The &quot;Kappa&quot; Architecture)"><node TEXT="You’ve hit the nail on the head regarding the &quot;Hot/Warm/Cold&quot; strategy."></node><node TEXT="Hot (Redis/Memorystore): Sub-millisecond latency. Only stores the latest feature vector for active users."></node><node TEXT="Warm (Bigtable/DynamoDB): 10ms latency. Stores recent history or less-frequently accessed features."></node><node TEXT="The Transition Mechanism (The &quot;How&quot;)"><node TEXT="In a Google-scale design, you don&apos;t move this data manually. You use Lifecycle Policies:"></node><node TEXT="Write-Through: When an event happens, write to the Stream (Kafka)."></node><node TEXT="Streaming Worker: Updates the Redis snapshot immediately."></node><node TEXT="Batch Sink: Every hour, a process takes the Kafka data and writes it to a Parquet file in the Cold Layer."></node><node TEXT="TTL Policy: The Cloud Provider (GCS/S3) automatically moves files from &quot;Standard&quot; to &quot;Archive&quot; after 365 days based on the file metadata."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Feature Schema."><node TEXT="In a massive system, you can&apos;t just change a feature from an int to a float without breaking 50 downstream models. You need a Schema Registry (using Protobuf) that enforces versioning."></node><node TEXT="If a Data Scientist wants to change a feature, they create v2."></node><node TEXT="v1 remains active until all models have migrated."></node></node><node TEXT="Reconciliation logic"><node TEXT="The Conflict: &quot;Fast Guess&quot; vs. &quot;Gold Standard&quot;"><node TEXT="Imagine a feature: user_click_count_24h."></node><node TEXT="The Stream (Online): Processes events as they arrive. It might count a duplicate click because of a network retry, or it might miss an event due to a temporary partition. It says the count is 10."></node><node TEXT="The Batch (Offline): Runs at midnight. It has the luxury of deduplicating every log and verifying every session. It calculates the actual count was 9."></node><node TEXT="If you just keep both rows based on timestamps, the model gets confused. Which one should it use for the &quot;Current&quot; value?"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Compaction &amp; Overwrite&quot;"><node TEXT="To solve this, we use a Lambda or Kappa architecture approach where the Batch job eventually &quot;corrects&quot; the Online store."></node><node TEXT="1. The Watermark System"><node TEXT="The system tracks a Watermark—a timestamp that says: &quot;Everything before 12:00 PM has been fully processed and cleaned by the Batch job.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Any query for data before the watermark goes to the Batch-calculated values."></node><node TEXT="Any query for data after the watermark adds the Stream-calculated increments on top of the last Batch state."></node></node><node TEXT="2. The Atomic Swap (The Transition)"><node TEXT="Once the Batch job finishes (the &quot;Gold Standard&quot; is ready):"></node><node TEXT="It writes the new, clean values into the Online Store (Redis/Bigtable)."></node><node TEXT="It updates the Watermark."></node><node TEXT="The Stream processor then &quot;trims&quot; its local state or resets its counters because the &quot;base&quot; has been updated."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Why this matters for your AI Interview"><node TEXT="If you explain this, you’re signaling that you understand Data Integrity at scale. Google interviewers will look for these three terms:"></node><node TEXT="Idempotency: Ensuring that if the Batch job runs twice, it doesn&apos;t double-count."></node><node TEXT="Exactly-once Semantics: The goal of the reconciliation (even if it&apos;s &quot;effectively once&quot; via deduplication)."></node><node TEXT="Late-Arriving Data: How do you handle an event that happened at 11:55 PM but didn&apos;t reach the server until 12:05 AM (after the batch job started)?"><node TEXT="Senior Answer: You keep a &quot;Look-back&quot; window where the batch job re-processes the last hour of the previous window to catch those stragglers."></node></node></node><node TEXT="&quot;Isn&apos;t this too complex?&quot;"><node TEXT="You can suggest a Pure Streaming (Kappa) approach:"></node><node TEXT="&quot;To keep it simple, we can treat the entire history as a stream. We use one single engine (like Apache Beam/Dataflow) to process both historical and real-time data. This way, there is only one codebase, and the &apos;transition&apos; is just the engine moving from reading files to reading a message queue.&quot;"></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="production" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Feature Monitoring &amp; &quot;Drift&quot; Detection"><node TEXT="In a traditional system, you monitor if the database is &quot;up.&quot; In AI, the database can be &quot;up&quot; but sending &quot;garbage&quot; data."></node><node TEXT="The Problem (Data Drift): If your model was trained on &quot;Average Transaction Amount&quot; in USD, but a bug starts sending values in Cents, your model’s accuracy will plummet, but your system won&apos;t &quot;crash.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Google Solution: An Observability Layer that calculates the statistical distribution (Mean, Median, StdDev) of features in real-time. If the distribution in production deviates significantly from the training distribution, it triggers an alert."></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Discovery&quot; Layer (The Feature Catalog)"><node TEXT="At a company with thousands of engineers, the biggest waste of money is redundancy."></node><node TEXT="The Problem: Ten different teams might all write their own &quot;User_Tenure_Days&quot; feature, each using slightly different logic (e.g., one counts from &quot;Account Created,&quot; another from &quot;First Purchase&quot;). This wastes storage and compute."></node><node TEXT="The Google Solution: A Searchable Metadata UI. Before a Data Scientist writes a line of code, they search the Feature Store for existing features. This requires a robust tagging system and &quot;lineage&quot; (showing exactly which model uses which feature)."></node></node><node TEXT="Multi-Region Replication &amp; Availability"><node TEXT="Google doesn&apos;t live in one data center. If a user is in Singapore, you can&apos;t fetch their features from a server in Virginia."></node><node TEXT="The Problem: How do you keep features &quot;consistent&quot; globally? If a user changes their &quot;Preferences&quot; in London, that feature needs to be updated in the Singapore cache instantly."></node><node TEXT="The Google Solution: Global Consistency via Spanner or Global Bigtable. You use a database that supports synchronous or near-synchronous replication."></node><node TEXT="Trade-off: You must decide between Eventual Consistency (Fast, but risk of old data) and Strong Consistency (Slower, but always accurate). For fraud detection, you choose Strong. For recommendations, Eventual is fine."></node></node><node TEXT="Privacy &amp; &quot;The Right to be Forgotten&quot; (GDPR)"><node TEXT="This is the hardest part of the &quot;Immutability&quot; we discussed."></node><node TEXT="The Problem: GDPR requires that if a user asks to be deleted, you must remove their data. But if your Offline Store is a massive, immutable Parquet log from the last five years, you can&apos;t easily &quot;delete&quot; one row without rewriting the whole file (which costs thousands of dollars in compute)."></node><node TEXT="The Google Solution: Tombstoning &amp; Compaction."><node TEXT="1. Mark the user&apos;s ID in a &quot;Deletion Map&quot; (Tombstone)."></node><node TEXT="2. The Feature Store API filters out any IDs in the map during retrieval."></node><node TEXT="3. During the next scheduled &quot;Batch Compaction&quot; (e.g., once a month), the system physically removes the data and rewrites the files."></node></node></node><node TEXT="On-Demand (On-the-Fly) Features"><node TEXT="Not everything should be pre-computed and stored."></node><node TEXT="The Problem: &quot;Distance to the nearest store&quot; cannot be pre-computed because it depends on the user&apos;s current GPS coordinates, which change every second."></node><node TEXT="The Google Solution: On-Demand Transformations. The Feature Store provides a runtime environment (like a Lambda function or a C++ snippet) that takes &quot;Online Features&quot; (Store Location) and &quot;Request Context&quot; (User GPS) and calculates the result at the moment of the request."></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="system design template"><node TEXT="steps and phases"><node TEXT="Phase 1: Clarification &amp; Scoping (5–7 Minutes)"><node TEXT="Goal: Define the &quot;Spec&quot; before writing code. Don&apos;t let the interviewer stay vague."></node><node TEXT="Functional Requirements: What are the 2 or 3 core user actions? (e.g., &quot;User can request a short URL,&quot; &quot;System redirects user&quot;)."></node><node TEXT="Non-Functional Requirements (The &quot;Senior&quot; Part)"><node TEXT="Scale: How many DAU (Daily Active Users)? (e.g., $100\text{M}$?)"></node><node TEXT="Consistency vs. Availability: Does it have to be 100% accurate every microsecond, or is &quot;Eventual Consistency&quot; okay for the sake of speed?"></node><node TEXT="Latency: What is the P99 target? ($&lt; 10\text{ms}$ vs. $&lt; 200\text{ms}$)."></node><node TEXT="Durability: Can we afford to lose a single data point if a server crashes?"></node></node></node><node TEXT="Phase 2: Back-of-the-Envelope Estimation (3–5 Minutes)"><node TEXT="Goal: Justify your hardware choices."></node><node TEXT="QPS (Queries Per Second) (Multiply by 2-5 for Peak QPS)."><node TEXT=""></node></node><node TEXT="Storage: $\text{Average Object Size} \times \text{Requests/Day} \times \text{Retention Period}$ (e.g., 5 years)."></node><node TEXT="Bandwidth: How many Gigabits per second are hitting the network card?"></node></node><node TEXT="Phase 3: The &quot;KISS&quot; High-Level Design (5–8 Minutes)"><node TEXT="Goal: Draw the &quot;Happy Path&quot; without getting bogged down in edge cases."></node><node TEXT="Contract Definition: Verbally or quickly sketch the API (e.g., POST /v1/resource { &quot;data&quot;: &quot;...&quot; })."></node><node TEXT="Boxes &amp; Arrows: * Load Balancer $\rightarrow$ API Gateway $\rightarrow$ Application Servers."><node TEXT="Storage Choice: Explain why you chose NoSQL (Scaling) vs. SQL (ACID)."></node><node TEXT="Cache Layer: Where does Redis sit?"></node></node></node><node TEXT="Phase 4: The Deep Dive (15–20 Minutes)"><node TEXT="Goal: Show your expertise. Pick 1 or 2 components to &quot;stress test.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Sharding Strategy: How do we split the database? (Hash-based? Range-based?)."></node><node TEXT="Distributed ID Generation: How do we generate unique IDs without a central bottleneck?"></node><node TEXT="Failure Modes: What happens when a Data Center goes offline?"></node><node TEXT="Data Integrity: How do we handle the &quot;Lambda&quot; transition or Reconciliation logic we discussed?"></node></node><node TEXT="Phase 5: Bottlenecks &amp; Scaling (5 Minutes)"><node TEXT="Goal: Break your own system before the interviewer does."></node><node TEXT="Hot Keys: How do you handle a &quot;Celebrity&quot; user hitting the same shard millions of times?"></node><node TEXT="Observability: How do we know the system is failing? (Metrics, Tracing, Logging)."></node><node TEXT="AI Context (If applicable): Mention Feature Drift monitoring or Vector Index re-sharding."></node></node><node TEXT="Phase 6: Conclusion (2 Minutes)"><node TEXT="Goal: Summarize the trade-offs."></node><node TEXT="&quot;We prioritized Low Latency over Strong Consistency because... but we added a Reconciliation worker to ensure data eventually settles.&quot;"></node></node></node><node TEXT="Pro-Tips for Google Success:"><node TEXT="Lead the Interview: Don&apos;t wait for &quot;What next?&quot; Say: &quot;I’ve defined the API and the high-level flow; would you like me to dive into the Database Sharding or the Kafka Partitioning logic next?&quot;"></node><node TEXT="State Your Trade-offs: There is no &quot;perfect&quot; system. Every choice (SQL vs. NoSQL) has a cost. Say it out loud."></node><node TEXT="Modular Thinking: Keep components decoupled. If you change the database, the API layer shouldn&apos;t care."></node></node><node TEXT="&quot;Senior&quot; cadence"><node TEXT="Clarify -&gt; Quantify -&gt; Architect -&gt; Stress-test."></node><node TEXT="how those points translate into high-level &quot;signals&quot; the interviewers are looking for." FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="The &quot;Ambiguity&quot; Phase (Clarification)"><node TEXT="Don&apos;t just ask for numbers; define the Contract."></node><node TEXT="The Goal: Turn a vague prompt into a specific API specification."></node><node TEXT="The Move: &quot;Before we draw the boxes, let’s define the interface. What are the inputs and outputs of our ShortenURL and RedirectURL endpoints?&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Scale&quot; Phase (Estimation)"><node TEXT="This isn&apos;t about being a human calculator; it’s about Hardware Realism."></node><node TEXT="The Goal: Prove that your design can physically fit on the machines."></node><node TEXT="The Move: &quot;If we have $100$ million writes per day, that&apos;s roughly $1,200$ QPS. At peak, we might hit $5,000$. A single SQL instance might struggle with that write volume, so we should consider a NoSQL store or a sharding strategy from the start.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Blueprint&quot; (High-Level to Detail)"><node TEXT="Use your Modular / KISS philosophy here."></node><node TEXT="The Goal: Build a system that is easy to maintain and decouple."></node><node TEXT="The Move: Start with a simple &quot;Happy Path&quot; (Load Balancer $\rightarrow$ App $\rightarrow$ DB). Once the logic is sound, then add the &quot;Complexity&quot; (Caches, Message Queues, Workers)."></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Stress Test&quot; (Trade-offs &amp; Hot Keys)"><node TEXT="This is where the L5/L6 distinction happens. A Junior says &quot;it works&quot;; a Senior says &quot;it breaks like this.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Hot Key Problem: If a world-famous celebrity posts a link, millions of people hit one shard at once."><node TEXT="Solution: Mention Adaptive Caching or Secondary Indexing."></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Wait&quot; Factor: If the database is slow, how do we prevent a &quot;Thundering Herd&quot; (where every client retries at once and crashes the server)?"><node TEXT="Solution: Mention Exponential Backoff and Jitter."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Closing Statement&quot; (Summary)"><node TEXT="Finish by circling back to your original trade-offs."></node><node TEXT="The Goal: Summarize why your design is the right balance of &quot;Simple&quot; and &quot;Robust.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Move: &quot;We prioritized Availability and Low Latency for the redirect path because a slow redirect feels like a broken app. We accepted Eventual Consistency for the analytics because being off by a few clicks for a minute doesn&apos;t break the core user experience.&quot;"></node></node></node><node TEXT=""></node></node><node TEXT="One Last AI Specific Tip"><node TEXT="Since you are in the AI Specialist track, always have a &quot;Plan B&quot; for when the AI is slow."></node><node TEXT="Example: &quot;While the RAG system retrieves context, we can show a cached &apos;Common Answer&apos; or a &apos;Thinking&apos; state to keep the UI responsive (P99 management).&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="hardware limit" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="latency"><node TEXT="The Architect&apos;s Move:"><node TEXT="If your &quot;Feature Store&quot; requires 5 sequential database lookups to return a result, and each takes 10ms, your P99 is already 50ms before any processing. This tells you that you must use parallel lookups or a flat Redis cache."></node></node><node TEXT=""></node></node><node TEXT="Throughput &amp; IOPS (The &quot;Pipe&quot; Problem)"><node TEXT="How much data can you push through the system before it chokes?"></node><node TEXT="Standard SSD: ~500 MB/s."></node><node TEXT="NVMe SSD: ~3–7 GB/s."></node><node TEXT="Network Card: 10 Gbps (Gigabit) is common ($\approx 1.25 GB/s$), but 100 Gbps exists in high-end clusters."></node><node TEXT="Database Writes (Disk): A single standard disk can handle roughly 1,000–5,000 Random Writes (IOPS). If you need 50,000 writes per second, you must shard or use a memory-first architecture (LSM-trees)."></node></node><node TEXT="GPU &amp; AI Constraints (The &quot;VRAM&quot; Problem)"><node TEXT="For an AI specialist, the bottleneck is rarely the CPU; it’s the GPU&apos;s memory and the &quot;interconnect&quot; between cards."></node><node TEXT="VRAM Capacity: An H100 has 80GB of VRAM. If your model + KV Cache (for LLM context) is 90GB, it cannot run on one card."><node TEXT="Estimation helps here: You must design for Model Sharding (Tensor Parallelism)."></node></node><node TEXT="NVLink vs. PCIe:"><node TEXT="PCIe Gen4: ~32 GB/s (The slow lane)."></node><node TEXT="NVLink: ~900 GB/s (The fast lane)."></node></node><node TEXT="The Architect&apos;s Move: If you are building a real-time RAG system that processes video, you can&apos;t move raw video frames over PCIe constantly; the bus will saturate. You must process as much as possible on the GPU or use heavy compression."></node></node><node TEXT="Memory vs. Disk (The &quot;Cost&quot; Problem)"><node TEXT="Estimation tells you if your design is financially viable."><node TEXT="RAM: Extremely fast, but expensive and &quot;volatile&quot; (data disappears on power loss)."></node><node TEXT="Object Storage (S3/GCS): Extremely cheap, virtually infinite, but slow ($&gt;100ms$ latency for the first byte)."></node></node><node TEXT="estimation scenario"><node TEXT=""></node></node></node><node TEXT="Why do we Estimate? (The &quot;Senior&quot; Signal)"><node TEXT="In an interview, when you say, &quot;Let&apos;s estimate the storage,&quot; you are proving you won&apos;t build a &quot;Gold-Plated&quot; solution that is too expensive, or a &quot;Paper-Thin&quot; solution that crashes on day one."></node><node TEXT="To Choose the Database: (If data &gt; 10TB and write-heavy $\rightarrow$ NoSQL/Sharded).To Choose the Protocol: (If sending 10k small updates per second $\rightarrow$ gRPC/Protobuf to save bandwidth)."></node><node TEXT="To Define the Cache Strategy: (Calculate the &quot;Cache Hit Ratio.&quot; If 20% of your data gets 80% of the traffic, you only need to cache that 20%)."></node></node><node TEXT=""></node></node></node></node>
</map>